1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:01,970
Why can one bulb turn off the whole

2
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:02,930
string of lights?

3
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:04,710
One tiny bulb fails...

4
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:06,930
and suddenly every light on the string

5
00:00:06,930 --> 00:00:07,630
goes dark.

6
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:09,680
Many light strings are wired in series.

7
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:12,160
That's one single loop, and the power has

8
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:14,190
to flow through every bulb in a row.

9
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:16,900
Inside each bulb, a thin wire glows.

10
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,320
If it snaps, the loop is broken, and

11
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:21,070
the power stops for all of them.

12
00:00:21,370 --> 00:00:23,870
That's why newer bulbs hide a tiny bypass

13
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:24,670
called a shunt.

14
00:00:25,170 --> 00:00:27,030
When the wire burns out, the power jumps

15
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:28,630
across it and keeps going.

16
00:00:28,970 --> 00:00:31,090
But a loose or missing bulb still breaks

17
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:33,090
the loop... so push that one back in,

18
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:34,690
and the whole string lights up.
